      {
        "canonical_field": "essay:generated:additional-information",
        "expected": "Two things that may not come through on a resume.\n\nFirst, most of what I build comes out of problems I actually ran into. The job-application agent started because I was doing the same repetitive browser work over and over, and the interesting part turned out to be deciding where automation should stop. I put every mutating capability behind fail-closed flags like SUBMIT_ENABLED, used SQLite as the single source of truth because browser state diverging across processes is how you accidentally submit something real, and made free-text and demographic fields impossible to auto-fill by design. That instinct about the boundary between machine action and human judgment is the thing I would bring to deployed software.\n\nSecond, a year-long placement matches how I work best. On the SAC Nexus anomaly-detection system I own the repo and CI scaffold, the event-ingestion pipeline, the versioned FastAPI service, the sequence models, and the retrieval-augmented explanation layer, and the parts that mattered most (alert prioritization, failure modes, documentation) only became clear after months with the system. I would rather own something end to end and live with its failure modes than pick up isolated tickets.",
        "observed": "Two things that may not come through on a resume.\n\nFirst, most of what I build comes out of problems I actually ran into. The job-application agent started because I was doing the same repetitive browser work over and over, and the interesting part turned out to be deciding where automation should stop. I put every mutating capability behind fail-closed flags like SUBMIT_ENABLED, used SQLite as the single source of truth because browser state diverging across processes is how you accidentally submit something real, and made free-text and demographic fields impossible to auto-fill by design. That instinct about the boundary between machine action and human judgment is the thing I would bring to deployed software.\n\nSecond, a year-long placement matches how I work best. On the SAC Nexus anomaly-detection system I own the repo and CI scaffold, the event-ingestion pipeline, the versioned FastAPI service, the sequence models, and the retrieval-augmented explanation layer, and the parts that mattered most (alert prioritization, failure modes, documentation) only became clear after months with the system. I would rather own something end to end and live with its failure modes than pick up isolated tickets.",
